 # Hymenoplasty (Hymen Reconstruction)
 
-**Hymenoplasty** (also called **hymenorrhaphy** or **hymen reconstruction**) sutures hymenal remnants or reconstructs a hymen, usually aiming to produce bleeding at later intercourse.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup> Bleeding is not assured: in one STSI series, 28/51 patients who reported intercourse reported bleeding at first intercourse.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> It is one of the least described vulvovaginal procedures in plastic surgery, with no surgical standard.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> In the single-center Lahlali 2021 retrospective series, 227 of 529 restoration procedures in 518 patients were hymenorrhaphies.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> No failure was reported in that group; those who reported intercourse within 15 days also reported bleeding, but the abstract does not specify how many were in that subgroup.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> For positioning versus other female cosmetic options see [Cosmetic Genital Surgery — Female](/docs/surgical-techniques/04l-cosmetic-genital-surgery/female-cosmetic).
+**Hymenoplasty** (also called **hymenorrhaphy** or **hymen reconstruction**) sutures hymenal remnants or reconstructs a hymen, usually aiming to produce bleeding at later intercourse.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup> Bleeding is not assured: in one STSI series, 28/51 patients who reported intercourse reported bleeding at first intercourse.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> It is one of the least described vulvovaginal procedures in plastic surgery, with no surgical standard.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> In the single-center Lahlali 2021 retrospective series, 227 of 529 restoration procedures in 518 patients were hymenorrhaphies.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> No failure was reported in that group; those who reported intercourse within 15 days also reported bleeding.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> For positioning versus other female cosmetic options see [Cosmetic Genital Surgery — Female](/docs/surgical-techniques/04l-cosmetic-genital-surgery/female-cosmetic).
 
 **Society positioning and ethical context.** ACOG Committee Opinion No. 795 (2020) classifies hymenoplasty as elective female genital cosmetic surgery. It states that such procedures are not medically indicated in the absence of a clinical indication, that women should be told high-quality data are lacking, and that patients should be assessed for body dysmorphic disorder (BDD) if indicated.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> The FIGO 2025 statement defines cosmetic genital surgery as aesthetic alteration in women without structural or functional abnormalities and says it is ethically inappropriate for obstetrician-gynecologists to recommend, perform or refer patients for those procedures.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup> Legal status varies by jurisdiction. In the United Kingdom, the Health and Care Act 2022 made carrying out, offering, or facilitating hymenoplasty an offence in England and Wales, Scotland, and Northern Ireland from 1 July 2022. Verify local law before offering or facilitating a procedure.<sup>[[12]](#ref12)</sup> A case report described a hymenorrhaphy that narrowed the introitus by suturing the labia minora; its authors classified that procedure under WHO definitions of female genital mutilation.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
 
